			<div class="content" id="post-content-4599360">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-1</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My primary problem with this starts with the Special Containment Procedures, and is the same as TL333s. The first paragraph of those is firmly Description, and it's both jarring and hard to read as formatted. As much as we want to protest that you should read the ConProcs first, the fact of the matter is that most people don't, and leaving Description info up there can result in a very confusing read. Even if you stick to the ideal of reading ConProcs first, their purpose is to act as a teaser and hint at various aspects of the SCP &#8230; which is defeated if you give everything away immediately.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-AI webcrawler ΛΞ-207 &quot;Wallbanger&quot; has been reassigned to trawl for any forum or social media postings indicating the current or prospective location of GoI-233.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is an unnecessarily long clause, with two &quot;or&quot;s clogging it up. The first could just be &quot;social media&quot;, since forums are social media. Also, I don't really see the Circus advertising on social media?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the anomalous nature of GoI-233's mode of travel, additional predictive analytics have been added to ΛΞ-207's codebase in an attempt to more successfully ascertain where they will appear next.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As with the previous, this is a bit unnecessarily long and could be easily made more concise. Consider that <tt>&quot;Due GoI-233's anomalous mode of travel, predictive analytics have been added to ΛΞ-207.&quot;</tt> has all the relevant information and is half the length. It seems this is a recurrent problem throughout the work. A lot of the times it's just two or three words that could be cut, but it adds up to bog the article down.</p>
<p>As a minor note, Black Highlighter doesn't handle Greek characters well, and they don't appear at the same text-size. It's best to avoid one of the two.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In the event that one or more of the SCP-5335 instances be contained, these procedures should be updated to include the contained instance's archived containment procedures.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This doesn't need to be said, it should be assumed.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>This massive cellular regeneration produces a fully-grown adult human and appears to select physical characteristics at random.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><em><strong>What</strong></em> massive cellular regeneration? This is the first time you talk about it, you can't introduce a concept with the pronoun &quot;this&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>SCP-5335 was originally brought to the Foundation's attention after the ongoing mutual exchange of files between the Foundation and the FBI:Unusual Incidents Unit began in 2███.1</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here's an egregious example of redundancy. You don't need to say &quot;the Foundation&quot; twice in this sentence. You need to rephrase this to only say it once.</p>
<p>Also, what is the point of only redacting the first digit? You explicitly say that further action is undertaken in 2012, so there's literally no ambiguity in the date. Did these files com from a hundred years in the future? Either redact the full date or don't redact the second digit.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Stewart: You are no longer an Agent of the FBI, SCP-5335-2.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The dialogue is a bit weak and sounds unnatural. &quot;Agent of the FBI&quot; parses wrong &#8212; nobody says, that, it's always &quot;FBI Agent&quot;. This continues throughout the article: I'm not a fan of much of the interplay, it just doesn't feel realistic to me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Stewart: Ok, McNeal then. I'd like to ask you some questions about how you came to be… like you are.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is really rather unprofessional dialogue: a doctor should have the ability to phrase a question better than this. It's just a hamfisted way for the anomaly to get in a joke.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interview was terminated shortly after that.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Terribly nonclinical. Just put the Closing Statement after your [END LOG] and drop this sentence: it'll do all the work for you by itself. It's already evident the interview ends by the fact we're no longer in an interview.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Interviewed: Retired FBI:UIU Agent Jackson Hooper</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The second interview is a completely different format from the first. The header information and the Begin/End tags look completely different.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>File selections</p>
</blockquote>
<p>All of the images in the image carousel in the UIU format broke and don't display.</p>
<hr />
<p>With a word on formatting: I'm very strongly not a fan of the combination of the Magnus Format with Black Highlighter. I don't think it looks good, especially the collapsible link colors.</p>
<p>Further, a lot of the design choices put no consideration towards margins/lost space. Both of the interviews nest the text within three borders, the last of which is firmly superfluous (there's nothing between it and the second border other than the link to close the collapsible). In parts of the UIU files, this gets as egregious as <em>four</em> layers deep when it goes Main Div -&gt; Collapsible -&gt; Tab -&gt; Second div. This clots up space on screen. I thought this was a problem on desktop, on mobile it looks like you're using nearly half the screen space to needless gutter.</p>
<p>With the image/page divs, this is also a large problem. The pages are quite compressed on the page, even viewing on desktop. I'd suggest moving the images <em>into</em> the <tt>class=&quot;page&quot;</tt> divs, which would greatly alleviate this problem, by letting the pages have their full width, rather than the smooshed width they need from the photos.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

